      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500">Source: IRCC processing-time data, last updated October 9, 2026, checked November 16, 2026. These are estimates for all provinces' nominees, not a guarantee for your file.</p>
      <p>
        The six-month gap between enhanced and base processing is a good reason to create an Express Entry profile if you qualify for one. For more detail, see <a href="/articles/how-long-to-get-pr-in-canada" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">how long it takes to get PR in Canada</a> and our <a href="/articles/ircc-processing-times-canada" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">IRCC processing times guide</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="changes" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">What Changed in the MPNP in 2025 and 2026</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>September 2, 2025:</strong> Employer Services reopened intake as the Employer Direct Initiative and launched the Temporary Resident Retention Pilot.</li>
        <li><strong>February 4, 2026:</strong> the retention pilot dropped hospitality and food services and added several skilled trades.</li>
        <li><strong>March 2, 2026:</strong> the MPNP stopped answering email inquiries; questions now go through webforms, and it will not extend LAA deadlines or edit EOIs for you.</li>
        <li><strong>April 14, 2026:</strong> Manitoba opted in to two federal Temporary Foreign Worker Program measures for employers outside the Winnipeg CMA (a 15% low-wage cap instead of 10%, and keeping current proportions above the cap), in effect until March 31, 2027.</li>
        <li><strong>May 1, 2026:</strong> the MPNP said it would hold targeted draws to invite holders of support letters under the 2025 temporary public policy who got work permits.</li>
        <li><strong>June 11, 2026:</strong> Career Employment Pathway candidates with six months of Manitoba work were told to move to the SWM Pathway, where graduates get priority selections.</li>
        <li><strong>July 6, 2026:</strong> IRCC signed the Manitoba Workforce Transition Bridge Temporary Public Policy (announced by the MPNP on August 5). It lets Manitoba issue up to 2,700 new support letters, only to people who already got one in 2024 or 2025, and extends their nomination deadline from December 31, 2026 to December 31, 2027. It is not open to new candidates.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="vs-bc" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Manitoba PNP vs BC PNP</h2>
      <p>
        Both programs mostly select people already working in the province, but the details differ. Manitoba's SWM Pathway rewards six months with your current Manitoba employer and gives no points for wage, so a modestly paid job in manufacturing, trades or health care can lead to an LAA. The BC PNP Skills Immigration streams score registrations with points that include your hourly wage and where in B.C. the job is, then invite by score or by occupation group. Manitoba also uniquely lets family and friends serve as your connection through SWO, although that route has been almost closed in 2026 draws. Our full <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-vs-manitoba-mpnp-2026" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">BC PNP vs Manitoba MPNP comparison</a> goes further, and you can test your B.C. side with the <a href="/bc-pnp-calculator" class="text-indigo-600 underline font-semibold">BC PNP Calculator</a> and check recent <a href="/bc-pnp-draws" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">BC PNP draws</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Do not apply to both at once and hope one sticks: the MPNP will not accept you if you have an active application in another provincial program, and work or study in another province costs 100 points each in Manitoba's EOI. If you are still choosing a province, read <a href="/articles/how-to-get-provincial-nomination-canada" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">how to get a provincial nomination in Canada</a> and compare it with the <a href="/articles/alberta-pnp-guide" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">Alberta PNP guide</a>.
      </p>

